  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nGOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery over the UP this \nmorning shows a band of stratus over the south-central and some \nisolated high cirrus elsewhere, but not much of any impact \nelsewhere. While a few patches of fog cannot be entirely ruled out, \nGLAMP guidance suggests probabilities of fog at any given place are \naround 10 percent, so fog impacts should be minimal. \n\nRAP Analysis shows 500mb ridge extending from the Gulf Coast to the \nNorthern Plains flanked by broad troughs over both the East and West \ncoast. The ridging advancing towards the Great Lakes will result in \nhigh pressure extending over the UP today and as a result, enough \nsubsidence to keep chances of precipitation 10% or less today. \n\nAttention turns upstream to a pair of troughs, a shortwave currently \nin eastern Colorado and a broader trough currently in Alberta. These \ntroughs will each bring precipitation to the Upper Midwest and \nnorthern Ontario regions, but neither individually track \nparticularly close to the UP, leaving the strongest forcing for \nthunderstorms elsewhere. Additionally, the CO shortwave looks to \ntrack directly south of the UP, interrupting the potential Gulf \nconnection of moisture transport to supply the Canadian trough. \nPWATs still surge to around 1.5 inches, which is above the 90th \npercentile of climatology, but the UP will miss out on the 2+ inch \nPWATs that the HREF projects as far north as Green Bay. With \nsuboptimal forcing and moisture supply, the rainfall totals have \ntrended down slightly. LREF 75th percentile rainfall (so, a little \nhigher than the average solution) by Sunday evening is about a \nquarter to a third of an inch, with the 90th percentile (reasonable \nwettest scenario) still showing half to three quarters of an inch of \nrain. On the dry end of scenarios, the 10th and 25th percentiles do \nat least show measurable rain for the entire UP, but it may not be a \nwetting rain as even the 25th percentile is around a tenth of an \ninch or less. While any rainfall will be welcome to the UP which is \nrunning a half inch to an inch below the August normal precipitation \npace, a little more precip would have been even more helpful. The \nthunderstorm threat remains present, but unlikely to be severe as \nEuro ensemble mean MUCAPE is around 100-350 J/kg, though peaking in \nthe overnight Saturday into Sunday period, so thunderstorms would \nhave to be well-forced and elevated in nature. With only moderate \nshear (around 30 knots mean sfc-500mb shear), severe weather remains \nunlikely.\n\nWhile ensemble spread sharply increases on the pattern that follows, \nit is generally expected that by Sunday evening and through Monday \nnight, ridging resumes in the wake of the trough, leading to dry \nweather. Ensembles are beginning to coalesce around a deep 500mb \nclosed low in the vicinity of Hudson Bay that will send a trough to \nthe Upper Great Lakes Tuesday. While there is considerable spread in \nthe details, confidence in at least a wetting rainfall (0.1\"/24hr) \nhas increased to 50-75%, with ridging behind the trough expected to \nbring more dry weather for the back half of the week.",
